BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Cannupa Hanska Luger’s REFLECT — Washington\, DC
DTSTART;TZID="UTC-05:00":20261105T130000
DTEND;TZID="UTC-05:00":20261105T170000
DTSTAMP:20261106T065326Z
UID:09bad66d372796e25ebde9808341@global-arts-culture
CATEGORIES:Arts & Culture\,Arts & Culture\,Washington\, DC\,United States
DESCRIPTION:REFLECT traces the evolution of artist Cannuap Hanska Luger’
 s Mirror Shield Project . In 2016\, when the Dakota Access Pipeline threat
 ened the waters of his ancestral homelands\, Luger circulated a video demo
 nstrating how to make “mirror shields” from inexpensive materials. Con
 ceived during the Indigenous-led resistance at Standing Rock\, North Dakot
 a\, the tutorial functioned as a call to collective action\, an invitation
  for people across the country to stand in solidarity with the Water Prote
 ctors. Carried by Water Protectors in frontline actions and collective for
 mations\, the mirrored surfaces offered protection while redirecting the f
 ield of vision. More than a thousand mirror shields were c\nCategory: Arts
  & Culture\nOriginal listing: https://calendar.gwu.edu/event/cannupa-hansk
 a-lugers-reflect
LOCATION:Flagg Building\, 500 17th Street NW\, Washington DC 20006
STATUS:CONFIRMED
URL:https://calendar.gwu.edu/event/cannupa-hanska-lugers-reflect
X-GLOBAL-CULTURE-REGION:us
X-GLOBAL-CULTURE-SOURCE:gwu-arts
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Cracks & Reforms: The 1920s Through the GW Collection — Washingt
 on\, DC
DTSTART;TZID="UTC-05:00":20261105T130000
DTEND;TZID="UTC-05:00":20261105T170000
DTSTAMP:20261106T065326Z
UID:26f196ebea1328c7bf5e731b3b16@global-arts-culture
CATEGORIES:Arts & Culture\,Arts & Culture\,Washington\, DC\,United States
DESCRIPTION:The 1920s emerged as a decade suspended between collapse and r
 econstruction\, an era marked by “cracks and reforms” that ushered in 
 a profound reconfiguration of the visual world.  During this period marke
 d by civil transformation and political upheaval\, artists\, journalists\,
  and makers had opportunities to reconstruct both individual and collectiv
 e identities. A century later\, Cracks & Reforms presents a distinct cross
 -section of this period\, revealing global currents of migration\, moderni
 zation\, and cultural redefinition through the lens of the GW Collection. 
 Students led by Associate Professor Bibiana Obler selected works to study 
 and exhibit\, their research eventually leading to unified t\nCategory: Ar
 ts & Culture\nOriginal listing: https://calendar.gwu.edu/event/cracks-refo
 rms-the-1920s-through-the-gw-collection
LOCATION:Flagg Building\, 500 17th Street NW\, Washington DC 20006
STATUS:CONFIRMED
URL:https://calendar.gwu.edu/event/cracks-reforms-the-1920s-through-the-gw
 -collection
X-GLOBAL-CULTURE-REGION:us
X-GLOBAL-CULTURE-SOURCE:gwu-arts
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Eric Gottesman’s NIGHT AND DAY — Washington\, DC
DTSTART;TZID="UTC-05:00":20261105T130000
DTEND;TZID="UTC-05:00":20261105T170000
DTSTAMP:20261106T065326Z
UID:9169446521d5d1ed686e7a0a3a01@global-arts-culture
CATEGORIES:Arts & Culture\,Arts & Culture\,Washington\, DC\,United States
DESCRIPTION:For Freedoms presents artist and educator Eric Gottesman’s N
 IGHT AND DAY which centers on Ethiopian journalist and author Baalu Girma
 ’s controversial 1984 Amharic novel Oromay \, written under the Derg reg
 ime and banned shortly after its publication. Drawing on Gottesman’s dee
 p engagement with the novel\, NIGHT AND DAY considers how artists create\,
  communicate\, and sustain cultural memory within and against conditions o
 f censorship\, political repression\, and state violence. The exhibition e
 xtends beyond the gallery through a library of banned books\, a self-guide
 d workshop station that invites visitors to respond to censorship through 
 writing and drawing\, and a public conversation on how artist\nCategory: A
 rts & Culture\nOriginal listing: https://calendar.gwu.edu/event/eric-gotte
 smans-night-and-day
LOCATION:Flagg Building\, 500 17th Street NW\, Washington DC 20006
STATUS:CONFIRMED
URL:https://calendar.gwu.edu/event/eric-gottesmans-night-and-day
X-GLOBAL-CULTURE-REGION:us
X-GLOBAL-CULTURE-SOURCE:gwu-arts
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Unfossilizing African Art — Washington\, DC
DTSTART;TZID="UTC-05:00":20261105T130000
DTEND;TZID="UTC-05:00":20261105T170000
DTSTAMP:20261106T065326Z
UID:6b8f2c9906dfa151bb2db2d29f08@global-arts-culture
CATEGORIES:Arts & Culture\,Arts & Culture\,Washington\, DC\,United States
DESCRIPTION:Presented after extensive research\, this exhibition recontext
 ualizes African material culture objects held in the GW Collection for nea
 rly 50 years. Led by Assistant Professor Clement Akpang\, 59 students from
  four seminar courses offered between Fall 2024 and Spring 2026\, conducte
 d original research to deepen understanding of the university’s holdings
  and update how we interpret and exhibit these objects. The exhibition fea
 tures 37 tangible heritage objects\, ranging from an Egúngún Paaka from 
 Yorubaland to a Kii [brass pipe]\, attributed to the Baumum people of Came
 roon\, accompanied by images and video that illuminate their rich historie
 s. Research on the entire collection is presented in an \nCategory: Arts &
  Culture\nOriginal listing: https://calendar.gwu.edu/event/unfossilizing-a
 frican-art
LOCATION:Flagg Building\, 500 17th Street NW\, Washington DC 20006
STATUS:CONFIRMED
URL:https://calendar.gwu.edu/event/unfossilizing-african-art
X-GLOBAL-CULTURE-REGION:us
X-GLOBAL-CULTURE-SOURCE:gwu-arts
END:VEVENT
